## Why does healthcare SEO have higher standards?

Healthcare SEO has higher standards because inaccurate health information can harm a person's wellbeing or decisions. Google places these pages in its Your Money or Your Life category. Generic copy gives readers and search systems no reliable way to judge who wrote the information. It also hides whether anyone reviewed it and when it was last updated.

Experience, expertise, authoritativeness and trust are often shortened to E-E-A-T. On a healthcare site those signals need visible evidence.

Practitioner profiles should state genuine qualifications, professional registrations, relevant roles and the pages each practitioner reviews. The site should also show ownership, contact details, editorial responsibility and accurate update dates.

The content itself needs a controlled process. A freelancer shouldn't invent clinical explanations from search results.

A clinic shouldn't publish pages that imply guaranteed results or hide the person responsible for the information.

My guide to [E-E-A-T in SEO](/blog/what-is-eeat-seo/) explains how evidence and presentation support visibility without a list of unsupported badges.

Technical quality matters as well. Broken booking paths, duplicate location pages, inconsistent practitioner details and missing canonicals weaken trust before anyone considers the wording. Higher standards apply across the whole journey.

## How does AI visibility work for private clinics?

Some patients now ask AI assistants as well as Google which clinic to choose. Generative engine optimisation (GEO) is the work of making a clinic easier for ChatGPT, Copilot, Gemini and Google's AI answers to find, check and cite. The same YMYL standards apply to these answers. An assistant has more reason to name a provider whose facts are clear, consistent and verifiable.

Illustrative examples of how patients phrase these questions:

- "Which private clinics near Reading offer same-week physiotherapy appointments?"
- "Is a private GP worth it in the UK, and which providers are well reviewed?"
- "Who are the best private dermatology clinics in London?"

These show question style only. They are not measured data.

| What AI assistants can check | What it means for your clinic |
| --- | --- |
| Clinician-reviewed content | Each clinical page names its author or reviewer, with a dated review that really happened |
| Professional registration | Practitioner profiles state the relevant register (GMC, GDC, NMC or HCPC) and the practitioner's own registration number, which patients can check on that register |
| CQC registration | Your CQC registration and current rating, where applicable, shown accurately and linked to your CQC listing |
| Consistent facts | The same clinic name, locations, services, opening hours and practitioners on your site, Google Business Profile and directories |
| Careful claims | Treatment pages that describe services without promising outcomes |

Advertising claims need the same care. Treatment pages must stay within the advertising rules set by the [ASA and CAP](https://www.asa.org.uk/). Your compliance team signs off that wording. I structure pages around approved wording and do not write treatment claims.

### How I measure AI visibility for clinics

I use a fixed prompt panel. The panel runs the same patient questions on the same assistants each month and records dates and conditions. I report where your clinic is named, which competitors appear instead and which sources the assistants cite. AI answers vary between runs and users. I report what I observe and never guarantee a mention.

The AI visibility audit is a £1,500 fixed-fee project delivered in 2 weeks.
 It sets your baseline, maps the sources cited for your services and gives you a prioritised plan. Implementation is scoped separately.

