## What does a healthcare SEO consultant do?

A healthcare SEO consultant organises verified information around the services and locations people are actually looking for. The work doesn't replace clinical governance. It makes that governance visible and keeps technical faults from obscuring it.

1. **Service and condition page architecture:** I map service pages, supporting condition information and location coverage so each has a distinct purpose. Clinicians decide what clinical information is accurate and appropriate. I define the search intent, page structure and internal links.
2. **Author and reviewer presentation:** I connect each clinical page to a real author or reviewer profile. Schema can describe the person, organisation and review relationship when the underlying facts exist. It must never manufacture expertise or imply a review that didn't happen.
3. **Local SEO for practices:** I align genuine locations, Google Business Profiles, service pages and contact information. [Local SEO](/services/local-seo/) matters most for practices where calls, directions and bookings begin in local search. It costs from £600 per month.
4. **Technical auditing:** I check crawlability, indexation, Core Web Vitals, duplicate pages, booking links, structured data and mobile behaviour. The technical SEO audit prioritises faults by their effect on visibility and enquiries.
5. **Reputation signals:** Reviews, policies, complaints information and professional details must be accurate and attributable. I structure those signals for clarity. I don't invent reviews or turn patient feedback into a claim about clinical outcomes.

## Who is healthcare SEO for?

Healthcare SEO is for private clinics, dental practices, aesthetics providers, physiotherapy practices, mental health practices, care providers and multi-location healthcare groups. The same search principles apply across these business models. The content responsibility never changes.

A dental practice may need strong service and location relationships. A multi-site provider needs clear ownership of each location and consistent practitioner information.

A care provider may need separate paths for families, commissioners and job applicants. An aesthetics site may have overlapping service names that require careful page separation.

The starting point is the provider's own services, practitioners, compliance process and Search Console data. Missing clinical review is a gap the provider must fix before any clinical page is publication-ready.

{% cta heading="Need a safer healthcare SEO structure?" text="Send me the site and your priority services. I will identify the visibility, trust and technical gaps without making clinical recommendations." /%}

## Who writes healthcare content and who signs it off?

A qualified practitioner with relevant expertise should author or review healthcare content. I provide search research, page structures, content briefs, internal linking plans, schema requirements and technical implementation guidance. The clinician supplies or signs off the clinical substance.

That division is deliberate. I can show which question a page needs to answer and how supporting information should be organised.

I cannot decide whether a statement about a condition, procedure, suitability or risk is clinically correct. The named practitioner and provider own that decision.

Advertising compliance also stays with the provider. ASA and CAP requirements sit within your compliance team's remit. So does your professional regulator's guidance from the GMC, GDC, HCPC or NMC.

I build the search and content system around the approvals, wording and evidence your team provides. I don't reinterpret those standards as medical or legal advice.

The publishing workflow should record who wrote the page, who reviewed it and when that review happened. Each date must describe a real event. A site-wide footer date isn't evidence that every clinical page was reviewed on that date.

